TI  - Randomized double-blind controlled clinical trial of the blood pressure-lowering effect of fermented milk with <i>Lactococcus lactis</i>: A pilot study
T2  - JOURNAL OF DAIRY SCIENCE
AB  - The blood pressure-lowering effect of fermented milk with Lactococcus lactis NRRL B-50571 was evaluated in a double-blind randomized controlled clinical trial with prehypertensive subjects. Participants were randomized into 2 groups (n = 18 each group): one group treated with fermented milk with Lactococcus lactis NRRL B-50571 and a control group treated with artificially acidified milk. Results revealed that during daily consumption of fermented milk for 5 wk, systolic [(116.55 +/- 12.26 mmHg vs. 124.77 +/- 11.04 mmHg) and diastolic blood pressure (80.7 +/- 9 vs. 84.5 +/- 8.5 mmHg)] from the fermented milk group was lower than the control group. Additionally, triglyceride, total cholesterol, and low-density lipoprotein in blood serum were lower in the fermented milk group than in the control group. Results demonstrated that daily consumption of fermented milk with Lactococcus lactis (NRRL B-50571) had a blood pressure-lowering effect on prehypertensive subjects. Regular consumption of this product may be used as a potential functional food.
