TI  - Fermented milk containing ACE-inhibitory peptides reduces blood pressure in middle aged hypertensive subjects
T2  - MILCHWISSENSCHAFT-MILK SCIENCE INTERNATIONAL
AB  - A placebo controlled study was conducted to test the effect of fermented milk Dahi containing Lactobacillus delbrueckii ssp. bulgaricus, Streptococcus salivarius ssp. thermophilus and Lactococcus lactis biovar. diacetylactis on 29 hypertensive subjects under 35-45y (A1), 45-55y (A2) and 55-65y (A3). A1, A2 and A3 received either 100ml test-fermented milk containing ACE-inhibitory biopeptides SKVYP, or placebo product for 4 weeks. Blood pressure, body weight, haemoglobin, serum cholesterol, random blood sugar and urine for glucosuria and proteinuria were examined initially, and at 2 weekly interval up to 8 weeks. Test group A2 (n=10), exhibited significant decline in systolic blood pressure during 2nd and 4th week by 7.2 +/- 2.1 mm Hg and 8.9 +/- 3.4 mm Hg. Their diastolic blood pressure reduced linearly up to 7.8 +/- 0.3 mm Hg at 8th week. Serum cholesterol of all 19 participants consuming test fermented milk reduced significantly up to 37.9 +/- 15.9mg/dL during 4th to 8th week. No adverse effects were noted with respect to other test parameters. These results suggest therapeutic potential of test fermented milk Dahi, for blood pressure reduction to middle aged hypertensive subjects.
