TI  - Isolation and antihypertensive effect of exopolysaccharides from <i>Lactobacillus casei</i> LC2W
T2  - MILCHWISSENSCHAFT-MILK SCIENCE INTERNATIONAL
AB  - Crude exopolysaccharides were prepared from cell-free whey of skim milk fermented by Lactobacillus casei LC2W by eliminating proteins with trichloroacetic acid and precipitated with ethanol. They were fractionated by anion exchange chromatography into three fractions (LCP1, LCP2 and LCP3) whose yields were 35.74%, 12.61% and 33.34%, respectively. The main fraction LCP1, with a molecular weight of 3.27x10(6) Da, was determined to be a homogeneous component by high-performance size-exclusion chromatography, and the purity was further testified by capillary electrophoresis. The antihypertensive effect of LCP1 was evaluated using spontaneously hypertensive rats (SHR) and normotensive Wistar rats. 16 SHR and 16 Wistar rats were randomized respectively into test groups and control groups. The SHR group was fed with the sample and maltodextrin, respectively, at the daily dosage of 15 mg/kg for seven days and Wistar rats were fed only once. Significant decreases in the systolic blood pressure of SHR were observed (p<0.01) and no change in heart rate. Nevertheless, LCP1 did not show any effect on the blood pressure and heart rate of Wistar rats. The results suggested the fraction LCP1 has significant anti hypertensive effect on rats.
