TI  - DIET-RELATED TOXEMIA IN PREGNANCY .1. FAT, FATTY-ACIDS, AND CHOLESTEROL
T2  - AMERICAN JOURNAL OF CLINICAL NUTRITION
AB  - Toxemia in pregnancy (preeclampsia) is characterized by a combination of at least 2 of these clinical symptoms: hypertension, edema and proteinuria. In 3 successive trials over 3 consecutive yr the dietary intake of a selected number of young pregnant women attending a Maternal and Infant Care Program at Tuskegee Institute were evaluated for total lipid, individual fatty acids and cholesterol. Women with toxemia or with any of the individual symptoms were identifed and women without toxemia or these symptoms served as controls. Results were variable from repetition to repetition in all but the toxemia group and the edema group. The consumption of total lipids and cholesterol was significantly greater in all 3 trials by the toxemia and edema groups. Total saturated, monounsaturated and polyunsaturated fatty acids were eaten in greater amounts. The greatest differences were in palmitic acid, stearic acid, oleic acid and linoleic acid. The proportion of unsaturated fatty acids consumed in all groups was very low. All differences could be attributed primarily to breakfast and dinner meals and were found in the milk, meat and egg food groups. Although statistical correlations were found between lipid intake and toxemia of pregnancy any specific relationship between the 2 is still unclear.
